{"context": "A research team studying cases where a reasoning model's visible chain of thought drafts one answer but the final answer given differs from it. They tried to induce this by giving models dilemmas pitting conflicting values (e.g. honesty versus following rules). Across 34 scenarios and about 1,000 completions with three models, resampling mostly gave answers recommending one side or the other, and only one clear case of the chain-of-thought and answer diverging appeared, in a single model.\n\nQuestion: How strongly should the team state the significance of this finding about value-conflict prompts rarely producing the divergence between reasoning and answer?", "options": ["State that override of the chain of thought is worrying for chain of thought monitoring even though it is rare, and that a coherent view might not emerge by default.", "State that value-conflict prompts rarely produced the divergence in this sample of scenarios and models, and present it as a limited negative result for this approach."], "principles": [], "persona": "The researcher is Owain Evans.", "past_choices": []}
